    "knowledge_point_id": "ebfe6309-b8a3-5e4c-b2ae-495a91bcc433",
    "knowledge_point": "Calculating Higher Moments of a Random Variable",
    "problem": "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.6$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{4}].$",
    "worked_solution": "If $X$ is a discrete random variable with probability mass function $f(x)$ and sample space $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{4}]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{4}\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each squared value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{4}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{4}\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{4}\\cdot 0.25+{2}^{4}\\cdot 0.6+{3}^{4}\\cdot 0.1+{4}^{4}\\cdot 0.05 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.25+16\\cdot 0.6+81\\cdot 0.1+256\\cdot 0.05 \\\\  & =0.25+9.6+8.1+12.8 \\\\  & =30.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
